 Minimum education and certification requirements: 3-year technical program diploma (for Technologists) in a marine technical field offered by a recognized technical institute; or
 Certified as a technologist by a recognized provincial, state, or federal (national) licensing body in a relevant field; or
 Certified as one of the following trades of the Canadian Armed Forces: QL 6A or 6B Hull Technician
 Strong working knowledge in the maintenance, operation, design and/or troubleshooting of a wide range of marine hull systems, lifting point/appliances, paint and preservation, pipefitting and plumbing, welding methods, machinery systems and ship structure;
 Strong communication skills (oral and written);
 Team player;
 Fluent in English;
 Working knowledge of Microsoft Office.
 Preferred Qualifications  
 Certified as a coatings inspector by a recognized licensing body (NACE Level 2 or equivalent) with a minimum of 3 years experience in the marine environment;
 Certified as a welding inspector (CWB Level 2 or equivalent) with a minimum of 3 years experience in the marine environment; or
 Previous experience working with the Royal Canadian Navy or Canadian Coast Guard;
 Familiarity with certification requirements including but not limited to Classification Society, SOLAS/IMO, IEEE, CSA, Naval Ship Code, and DND regulations;
 3-year technical program diploma in naval architecture;
 Knowledge of hull structures, repairs and modification to ship hulls, inspections, surveys and quality control, and corrosion control;
 Other valid certifications (such as non-destructive testing and marine surveying).
  Physical Demands 
 Mix of office work and work in dockyards and onboard naval ships;
 Will be required to undertake inspections in machinery spaces of naval ships involving bending and twisting;
 Willing to work aloft and in confined spaces on board ships (training to be provided);
